# Supabase — get these from your project's Settings > API page
# https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/<your-project>/settings/api
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGci...

# The canonical origin this deployment publishes as, e.g.
# https://apextrainingcalendar.vercel.app — no trailing slash or path.
# Read by BOTH sides (api/_lib/oauth/common.ts and src/lib/origin.ts), which
# is why it carries the VITE_ prefix despite also being read server-side: it
# is a public value, and one variable can't drift from itself.
#
# Everything a client stores comes from it: the OAuth issuer and endpoints,
# the MCP endpoint URL, the ICS feed URL, password-reset redirects. Without
# it those follow the request's Host, so a user on a Vercel deployment URL
# (apex-training-<hash>.vercel.app) copies that frozen, SSO-walled host into
# Claude or their calendar app.
#
# Set it in Vercel for PRODUCTION only. Leave it unset locally and for preview
# deployments — dev, e2e, and previews want their own host, and a preview that
# advertised the production origin would send its own OAuth flow to prod.
VITE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

# Service-role key — SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Never prefix with VITE_ (Vite would
# bundle it into client JS). Used by /api/* serverless functions to bypass
# RLS for writes. Also needs to be set in Vercel project env vars.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGci...

# Anthropic keys are PER-USER since phase 11 — each user saves their own
# key via Profile → AI Coach in the app (stored server-side in the
# user_api_keys table). No ANTHROPIC_API_KEY env var is read anymore.

# Encrypts stored user API keys at rest (AES-256-GCM in the API layer, see
# api/_lib/keyCrypto.ts). SERVER-SIDE ONLY — set in Vercel too. Any long
# random string (16+ chars); generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Unset = keys stored plaintext (a loud server-log warning on every save);
# existing plaintext rows are re-encrypted on first read once this is set.
# Rotating it invalidates saved keys — users just re-save theirs.
API_KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=

# Multi-user (phase 9): the auth user whose recurring workouts seed new
# accounts via /api/template-copy. Server-side only; set in Vercel too.
# Falls back to the profiles row with is_template_source = true.
SEED_SOURCE_USER_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Review emails + nightly cron (phase 12 / phase 29). SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# CRON_SECRET is the bearer token guarding /api/review-cron and
# /api/provider-cron. Vercel sends it on scheduled runs, so set it in Vercel;
# locally it is only needed to curl a cron by hand (README → Review emails,
# Watch sync). GMAIL_USER / GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD send the review emails over
# Gmail SMTP: the address (also the From) and a 16-char app password from a
# 2-Step-Verification account. All three unset = crons answer 401 and no mail
# goes out; the app itself is unaffected.
CRON_SECRET=
GMAIL_USER=
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=

# COROS sync (phase 27). SERVER-SIDE ONLY — set in Vercel too. The client id
# is a public OAuth client (PKCE, no secret) minted once via COROS's open
# dynamic-client-registration endpoint; run `node scripts/coros-spike.mjs
# discover` to register one, using your deployment's callback as the
# redirect URI. COROS_REDIRECT_URI must exactly match a registered value,
# e.g. https://<your-domain>/api/provider-callback (localhost variants are
# accepted for dev). Both unset = the COROS section shows "not configured".
COROS_CLIENT_ID=
COROS_REDIRECT_URI=
